How this is built

Same pipeline and same public repository as the overview page. “Difference” is the absolute gap between this pipeline and INEGI’s own published value for the same series. Across all 20 series and 85 quarters checked, the median gap is 0.013 pp and the largest is 0.216 pp. Gaps of this size drift slightly by sub-period, which is consistent with revisions to the survey’s expansion factors after recalibration to updated population projections, and the tolerance is set at 0.25 pp. 2020-Q2 is excluded from the comparison throughout: the BIE splices in the ETOE telephone survey for that quarter, and this pipeline leaves it as a genuine gap. Quarter-over-quarter and year-over-year changes are computed against the exact matching period, not by row position, so the 2020-Q2 gap (ETOE, not comparable) never produces a change calculated across two quarters of distance. The “biggest mover” highlights use a 95% significance filter (quarter-over-quarter or year-over-year change greater than 1.96 combined standard errors) computed by treating the two periods’ estimates as independent; ENOE’s rotating panel means adjacent quarters actually share some of the same respondents, so this is a conservative approximation — a real quarter-over-quarter move is a little more likely to be flagged as significant than this threshold gives it credit for, not less. The percent figure in parentheses next to each change is that change divided by the prior period’s own value, not a second percentage-point figure — a 0.1 pp move on a 2% base is a much bigger relative swing than the same 0.1 pp on a 60% base, which the parenthetical is there to make visible. TSUB and TCCO don’t appear in the by-group section: no state/demographic breakdown has been computed for those two yet. Source: ENOE microdata, INEGI, 2005–2026.

