How this is built

Same pipeline and same public repository as the overview page. TD, TDAMPL, and SUBUTIL are the same underutilization ladder shown there, broken down further by the cuts INEGI doesn’t publish. The shaded band and error bars are a 95% confidence interval (±1.96 standard errors) from the ENOE’s complex survey design. The state breakdown uses the 32 state-level estimates the quarterly ENOE supports (unlike the monthly release). 2020-Q2 (the ETOE, a phone-based substitute survey run during the strictest pandemic lockdown) is excluded entirely from the series rather than shown as zero or interpolated — its methodology isn’t comparable to the regular ENOE, so it’s a genuine gap, not missing data.

How a narrative spreads
Narrative Economics takes its central mechanism from epidemiology. Kermack and McKendrick (1927) split a population into three groups: those who have not caught a disease, those who have it and pass it on, and those who have stopped transmitting. Shiller applies the same three groups to a story. Some people have not heard it, some are telling it, and some heard it and no longer repeat it.
Two rates move people between the groups. The contagion rate c sets how fast tellers pass the story to people who have not heard it. The forgetting rate r sets how fast tellers lose interest and stop. Their ratio is the basic reproduction number, R0 = c / r: the number of new tellers one teller produces while almost everyone is still susceptible. Above one the story becomes an epidemic. Below one it dies out.

sirSemilla = 0.001
sirDias = 200
sirPaso = 0.05

// Explicit Euler. S + I + R holds at 1 to within 1e-14 everywhere on the
// slider grid. Integrated to convergence, a step of 0.05 lands within 0.07 pp
// of the analytic final size, which for a seeded run is
// S_inf = S_0 exp(-R0 (1 - S_inf)) with S_0 = 1 - sirSemilla, not the
// S_0 = 1 form: at R0 = 1 the two differ by about 4 pp, so checking against
// the wrong one invents an integrator error that is not there.
// What the run does NOT do is reach that asymptote in every setting. Just
// above R0 = 1 the epidemic is still under way on day sirDias and the final
// state sits far from S_inf. That is the fixed window, not the step size, and
// refining the step does not move it. It is why the reading says "still
// rising" instead of naming a peak there, and why it always says "by day
// sirDias" rather than reporting a total.
// Days are recorded as k / porDia rather than k * sirPaso so the axis carries
// exact integers.
sirSimular = (c, r) =&gt; {
  const porDia = Math.round(1 / sirPaso);
  let S = 1 - sirSemilla, I = sirSemilla, R = 0;
  const filas = [{ dia: 0, S, I, R }];
  for (let k = 1; k &lt;= sirDias * porDia; k++) {
    const nuevos = c * S * I * sirPaso;
    const salen = r * I * sirPaso;
    S -= nuevos;
    I += nuevos - salen;
    R += salen;
    if (k % porDia === 0) filas.push({ dia: k / porDia, S, I, R });
  }
  return filas;
}

sirEtiqueta = ({
  S: "Have not heard it",
  I: "Telling it",
  R: "Stopped telling"
})
sirDominio = [sirEtiqueta.S, sirEtiqueta.I, sirEtiqueta.R]
sirColores = ["var(--teal)", "var(--clay)", "var(--ink-soft)"]

sirLargo = traza =&gt; traza.flatMap(d =&gt; [
  { dia: d.dia, grupo: sirEtiqueta.S, valor: d.S },
  { dia: d.dia, grupo: sirEtiqueta.I, valor: d.I },
  { dia: d.dia, grupo: sirEtiqueta.R, valor: d.R }
])

sirResumen = (c, r) =&gt; {
  const traza = sirSimular(c, r);
  let pico = traza[0];
  for (const d of traza) if (d.I &gt; pico.I) pico = d;
  const fin = traza[traza.length - 1];
  return { traza, R0: c / r, pico, alcance: 1 - fin.S, fin };
}

// The three series are labelled at the end of their own line, so two labels
// collide whenever two curves finish together: I and R both sit at zero when
// R0 &lt; 1, and S and R meet when half the population has heard the story. This
// pushes them apart by a minimum gap in data units. It only ever moves labels
// up, and the top one can rise by at most two gaps, so it cannot leave the
// frame.
sirSeparar = (puntos, minimo) =&gt; {
  const orden = puntos.map(d =&gt; ({ ...d })).sort((a, b) =&gt; a.valor - b.valor);
  for (let i = 1; i &lt; orden.length; i++) {
    if (orden[i].valor - orden[i - 1].valor &lt; minimo) {
      orden[i].valor = orden[i - 1].valor + minimo;
    }
  }
  return orden;
}

sirPorciento = v =&gt; (v * 100).toFixed(1) + "%"

// The largest I in the trace is not always the peak of the curve. The window
// is fixed at sirDias, and for the six reachable settings just above R0 = 1
// (c/r of 0.11/0.10, 0.12/0.10, 0.12/0.11, 0.13/0.12, 0.14/0.13, 0.15/0.14)
// the story is still spreading when the window ends, so the maximum falls on
// the last day. Calling that a peak would name the edge of the frame as an
// event the chart does not show: the curve leaves the plot still climbing.
// Below R0 = 1 there is no peak either, only the seed decaying from day 0.
// So the reading reports a peak only where the curve has actually turned.
sirLectura = (c, r) =&gt; {
  const x = sirResumen(c, r);
  const partes = [
    `R0 = ${x.R0.toFixed(2)}`,
    x.R0 &gt; 1 ? "it spreads" : "it dies out"
  ];
  if (x.R0 &gt; 1) {
    partes.push(x.pico.dia === sirDias
      ? `still rising on day ${sirDias}`
      : `peak ${sirPorciento(x.pico.I)} on day ${x.pico.dia}`);
  }
  partes.push(`${sirPorciento(x.alcance)} have heard it by day ${sirDias}`);
  return html`&lt;div class="data-meta"&gt;${partes.join(" · ")}&lt;/div&gt;`;
}

// A display cell that is only a cell name renders Observable's inspector, so
// every visual output here is a function called from its own cell (CLAUDE.md
// section 13, bug 0).
sirGrafica = (c, r) =&gt; {
  const x = sirResumen(c, r);
  const largo = sirLargo(x.traza);
  const etiquetas = sirSeparar([
    { grupo: sirEtiqueta.S, valor: x.fin.S },
    { grupo: sirEtiqueta.I, valor: x.fin.I },
    { grupo: sirEtiqueta.R, valor: x.fin.R }
  ], 0.065).map(d =&gt; ({ ...d, dia: sirDias }));

  return Plot.plot({
    width: 680,
    height: 320,
    marginTop: 24,
    marginLeft: 46,
    marginBottom: 32,
    marginRight: 150,
    style: {
      background: "transparent",
      color: "var(--ink-soft)",
      fontFamily: "IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif",
      fontSize: "12px"
    },
    x: { label: null, domain: [0, sirDias] },
    y: { label: null, domain: [0, 1] },
    color: { domain: sirDominio, range: sirColores },
    marks: [
      Plot.gridY({ stroke: "var(--rule)" }),
      Plot.lineY(largo, {
        x: "dia", y: "valor", z: "grupo", stroke: "grupo",
        strokeWidth: 2, strokeLinejoin: "round", strokeLinecap: "round"
      }),
      Plot.text(etiquetas, {
        x: "dia", y: "valor", text: "grupo", fill: "grupo",
        textAnchor: "start", dx: 8, fontSize: 13, fontWeight: 500
      }),
      // The tip's data is the union of every series the chart draws, and its
      // title names the series it is describing (CLAUDE.md section 13).
      Plot.tip(largo, Plot.pointerX({
        x: "dia", y: "valor",
        title: d =&gt; `${d.grupo}\nday ${d.dia}\n${sirPorciento(d.valor)}`
      })),
      Plot.axisX({ stroke: "var(--rule)", tickSize: 0, ticks: 5 }),
      Plot.axisY({
        stroke: "var(--rule)", tickSize: 0, ticks: 5,
        tickFormat: d =&gt; (d * 100).toFixed(0) + "%"
      })
    ]
  });
}






























































































